Telegram Setup
Follow these steps to connect the workflow to your Telegram channel.

1. Create a Telegram Bot
Open Telegram and search for @BotFather.
Send /newbot and follow the instructions to choose a name and username.
BotFather will give you a bot token. Keep it private, as anyone with this token may be able to control your bot.

2. Create a Telegram Channel
If you don’t already have a channel:
Open Telegram and create a new channel.
Choose a channel name and set it to public or private, depending on your preference.
Open the channel settings and select Administrators.
Add your bot as an administrator and allow it to post messages.

3. Get Your Channel Chat ID
You need the channel’s chat ID, not your personal Telegram chat ID or the bot’s ID.
Add your bot to your channel as an administrator.
Post a test message in the channel, such as test.
Open the following URL in your browser, replacing YOUR_BOT_TOKEN with your actual bot token:
https://api.telegram.org/botYOUR_BOT_TOKEN/getUpdates
Look for the channel_post section in the response.
Find the chat object and look for its id field. This is your channel’s chat ID. It often starts with -100.
For example, if the response contains:
"chat":{"id":-1001234567890, "title": "My News Channel", "type": "channel"}
Your channel chat ID is -1001234567890.
Note: If the result is empty, or you don’t see a channel_post, check that your bot is an administrator and that a new channel post has been made. If a webhook is already configured for the bot, getUpdates won’t work until that webhook is removed.
Security: Your bot token is private. The URL above contains your token, so don’t share it or include it in screenshots or GitHub files.

4. Configure n8n
Open the workflow in n8n.
Open the Telegram node and create a Telegram API credential.
Enter your bot token and save it.
Set the Telegram chat ID to your channel’s chat ID.
Test the workflow and confirm that a message arrives in your channel.

5. Customising the Workflow

Using the workflow for other topics

By default, the workflow assigns priority labels to cybersecurity news based on keywords in the article title. This classification is keyword-based and may not always reflect the actual severity of an article.

If you want to use the workflow for general news or other topics without cybersecurity priority labels:

Open the workflow in n8n.
Find the Code in JavaScript node.
Replace its JavaScript code with the alternative code below.
Save the workflow and test it.


### Alternative JavaScript Code

Use this code if you want to remove the cybersecurity priority labels and send general news instead.

const title = $json.title;
const link = $json.link;
const content = $json.content;
const website_tag = $json.website_tag;

const date = new Date($json.date).toLocaleString("en-GB", {
  day: "2-digit",
  month: "short",
  year: "numeric",
  hour: "2-digit",
  minute: "2-digit"
});

const shortContent =
  content.length > 450
    ? content.substring(0, 450) + "..."
    : content;

const message = `
📌 ${title}

${date}
${website_tag}

📄 Description:
${shortContent}

🔗 Read more:
${link}
`;

return {
  json: {
    message
  }
};

